# Build output. The binary is ~19M and is reproducible from source with
# `go build -o vibekiller .`, so it does not belong in history.
#
# Both patterns are root-anchored, and that is load-bearing. A bare
# `vibekiller` has no slash in it, so git matches it against every file AND
# directory of that name at any depth -- which silently excludes
# rules/vibekiller/, the entire embedded Semgrep rule pack. The tree still
# looks complete locally because the files are right there; it is the clone
# that fails, at `go:embed vibekiller/*.yaml` with "no matching files".
/vibekiller
/vibekiller.exe
/dist/
*.test
*.test.exe

# Run output.
#
# This is the important one. runs/<id>/ holds findings.json, which carries
# file paths, code snippets and request/response evidence from whatever the
# operator scanned. Vibe Killer's whole premise is that results stay local;
# committing a run directory would undo that for anyone who pushes the repo,
# and a fixture scan is cheap to regenerate anyway.
/runs/
runs/
history.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Fixture dependencies. The fixtures are deliberately vulnerable stubs; their
# manifests are committed, so an installed tree is disposable and should never
# be pushed under a repo whose README tells people to scan it.
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
venv/
.venv/

# Local operator config. vibekiller.config.yaml is committed as the worked
# example; a real one names real hosts, so keep overrides out of the repo.
vibekiller.local.yaml
vibekiller.config.local.yaml
.env
.env.*

# Editor and OS noise.
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
